  "full_title": "A Lady with a Squirrel and a Starling (Anne Lovell?)",
  "short_title": "A Lady with a Squirrel and a Starling (Anne Lovell?)",
  "object_name": "Picture",
  "style": "high Renaissance",
  "material": "oil on wood",
  "dimensions": "56 × 38.8 cm",
  "production_date": "probably 1527-8",
  "brief_description": "A solemn woman wearing a soft cap of dense white fur sits with a red squirrel in her lap and a glossy-feathered starling at her shoulder. Common pets in the fifteenth-century, these animals also have a symbolic meaning and serve as clues to the sitter's identity. She is thought to be Anne Lovell, whose husband, Sir Francis Lovell, was employed at the court of Henry VIII, King of England.\n\nThe starling is probably intended as a rhyming pun of East Harling, where the family had recently inherited a large estate. Squirrels nibbling on nuts feature on the heraldry of the Lovell family: the windows of the church at East Harling include two of the family's coats of arms in stained glass, each showing six red squirrels. The commission may commemorate the birth of a son to the couple in the spring of 1526, but it also showed off their new status as wealthy landowners.",
  "accession_date": "1993-01-01",
